A domain is a human-readable address used to identify a website or resource on the internet. For example, unemployengineer.works is a domain. Domains are part of the Domain Name System (DNS), which translates these human-readable names into IP addresses that computers use to locate each other.

Explanation of Terms:
Root Domain:
    The root domain is the highest level of a domain name. In your example, unemployengineer.works is the root domain. It consists of:
    Second-level domain (SLD): unemployengineer
    Top-level domain (TLD): .works

Subdomain:
A subdomain is a prefix added to the root domain to create a separate section or environment of the website. For example:
    site1.stage.unemployengineer.works is a subdomain of unemployengineer.works.

Here:
site1 and stage are subdomains.
unemployengineer.works is the root domain.

Hierarchy Breakdown:

Root Domain: unemployengineer.works
Subdomain: site1.stage.unemployengineer.works
site1 is a sub-subdomain of stage.unemployengineer.works.
stage is a subdomain of unemployengineer.works.

Why Use Subdomains?
Subdomains are often used to organize different sections of a website or to create staging environments. For example:
stage.unemployengineer.works could be a staging environment for testing.

site1.stage.unemployengineer.works could be a specific site or application within the staging environment.

Record Types: A and CNAME

A Record:
An A (Address) record maps a domain name to an IPv4 address. It is used to point a domain or subdomain to the IP address of the server hosting the website or resource.

Example:
unemployengineer.works -> 192.168.1.1

CNAME Record:
A CNAME (Canonical Name) record maps a domain name to another domain name. It is used to alias one domain to another, allowing you to point multiple domains or subdomains to the same destination.

Example:
www.unemployengineer.works -> unemployengineer.works
site1.stage.unemployengineer.works -> stage.unemployengineer.works



